Haakon Studebaker wrote:
> But it seems that Microsoft is quicker with the turnaround time on patches
> for their operating system than the DevTeam is with the next NetHack.

When the Devteam does Nethack as their full time job and are paid
>$70,000 each to do it, backed by the full resources of the world's largest software company,  then we can complain about their producing patches far more slowly than Microsoft does for Microsoft's flagship products.

Since it is done in spare time by people who have more than enough work
to keep them busy, let's be happy with what we have :).   We're getting
the service we paid for.

'Nother point;  Nethack doesn't really need that many patches. The
game, unlike Longhorn or XP, has been in constant development for 20
years and really isn't very ambitious as far as computing resources go.
  It's very stable.  I haven't had the game crash on me in five years,
and I've never seen the dungeon collapse.   Any patches to it tend to
be "nice-to-have" YANIs rather than "fix it or the program crashes"
problems.    No current Microsoft product is anywhere near that level
of maturity.

Final point:   Last I checked,  no one's using Nethack for
time-critical,  business-critical applications.   Quite a few Microsoft
products are.  As a result, patching Nethack isn't nearly as critical.
>
> Are roguelikes dead and has Netcraft confirmed it?

If you mean "passed over by the mainstream community in favor of Diablo
and other games with graphics", I would agree.

However, if you mean "no one plays it",  I would disagree.   It is
still a fun game and more people come in all the time. So I see Nethack
as  a minor player on the world stage that a small group of adherents
nonetheless find immensely satisfying.

